      "title": "Newton's First Law: The Law of Inertia",
      "renderer": "wan_video",
      "narration": "Newton's First Law of Motion is often called the Law of Inertia. It states that an object at rest will stay at rest, and an object in motion will stay in motion with the same speed and in the same direction, unless acted upon by an unbalanced force. Let's break that down. 'Inertia' is the resistance any physical object has to any change in its state of motion. Think about a heavy book on a table. It doesn't spontaneously start moving. It stays at rest because of inertia. To make it move, you have to apply a force – a push. Now, imagine a hockey puck gliding on a perfectly smooth, endless sheet of ice. If there were no friction and no air resistance, the puck would glide forever at a constant velocity. It wouldn't slow down, speed up, or change direction. This is the second part of the law. The key phrase here is 'unbalanced force.' If the forces on an object are balanced, like a book on a table where gravity pulls down and the table pushes up with equal force, there is no change in motion. An unbalanced force is needed to overcome inertia and change the object's velocity, which means to accelerate it.",
